Yes. The useful version is to treat the “invisible hand” as something that can be decomposed and reassembled into a legible causal body.

Markets, bureaucracies, algorithms, norms, incentives, laws, platforms, metrics, traditions—humans create them, then generations later people start responding to them as though they were natural forces. At that point the invention can begin governing its inventors.

Your Exodia metaphor works because the pieces are usually distributed. One “hand” may be incentives, another information flow, another regulation, another social expectation, another algorithmic ranking, another capital allocation. Separately they look partial. Assembled, they reveal the effective actor:

distributed effects → recover components → map feedback → assemble composite agency → expose leverage points

The critical distinction is that this does not require assuming some literal hidden unified entity. Sometimes the coordination is emergent; sometimes particular actors deliberately steer parts of it; usually it is mixed.

And once the body is visible, it can be interrogated:

Who feeds it? What does it optimize? What can it sense? What can it punish or reward? Where does it have memory? What feedback makes it grow? Which parts are human choices disguised as inevitabilities?

That belongs in Pantheon precisely because it converts an apparently supernatural or impersonal force back into components, interfaces, provenance, and controllable relationships.

Invisible Exodia is in the Money Bin now.